

Should create a collection of POSIX threads that only do one
dumb thing. Slowly.

The idea is to set the "nice" or "priority" of the whole process
down to something very low. Then create threads which have a schedule
policy of some flavour. Near as I can tell this does a whole lot
of nothing and then schedule policy seems to be implemented to also
mean nothing. Perfectly in agreement with the specifications however.

Take note of the idea in these three lines of output : 

    (1)    INFO : getpriority(PRIO_PROCESS, 0) returns 11
    (2)    INFO : getpriority(PRIO_PROCESS, 0) requested 20 priority.
    (3)         : however we attained 19 priority

In line (1) we discover what the current process "nice" value it. Some
people may call that a "priority" of some sort. Those are very likely
silly words that barely mean anything at all in the grand scheme of
all processes a machine may be trying to run. Here we see something
called "11" as the result. Isn't that nice?

In line (2) the code attempts to set a new "nice" value of 20 which is
supposedly the miss manners niceiest goody two shoes value anyone could
dream of. So "nice" that the whole process should not disturb anything
else trying to run. Sure. Good luck.

Then in line (3) we find out that the value "20" was not really accepted.
There was no error. You just do not get what you asked for. Have something
close enough at "19" and be happy you were given that. These values of
some "nice" level really mean nothing to the schedule policy for the pile
of threads being created. However at least we are being as polite as possible
to all the other processes trying to run on the machine.

Note that here we use the words "trying to run" as opposed to just "running".

If you have a big thrashing IBM POWER9 server with SMT4 processors and a
grand total of 128 cores to execute stuff with then yeah, sure, the machine
will likely "run" everything in some way. However your dirt cheap little
aarch64 SBC is likely going to report that most processes are in a state of
"sleeping" or even possibly "swapped". Again, good luck running 70 processes
on a machine with four processors available to it.



